  "id": "Compound::DB00977 - Disease::DOID:10283",
  "verbalization": "Ethinyl Estradiol is implicated in the treatment of prostate cancer through multiple indirect pathways, with the core mechanism involving its relationship with estrone. The evidence suggests that Ethinyl Estradiol, through its resemblance to Equilin and Estrone, ultimately contributes to the treatment of prostate cancer by influencing the estrogen receptor pathway. This is supported by the fact that Estrone, which is closely related to Ethinyl Estradiol, directly treats prostate cancer. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential role of estrogen-related compounds in modulating the estrogen receptor, which is a well-established mechanism in prostate cancer therapy. The presence of multiple indirect links, including the involvement of 3-hydroxy steroid and estrogen, underscores the complexity of the relationship and the importance of considering the broader context of estrogen signaling in cancer biology.",
